    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Sasanian Empire ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from 224 to 651, a centralised Zoroastrian monarchy that was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>'s great rival for four centuries.</li><li>Its armoured cavalry, fixed land tax and court culture shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and, above all, the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphates</a> that followed.</li><li>A ruinous war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and a succession crisis left it defenceless against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> that ended it by 651.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a> captured the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valerian">Valerian</a> at Edessa in 260, the only time a reigning Roman emperor was taken prisoner by a foreign power.</p></aside>
    </section>

