    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ardashir-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="Ardashir I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinar_of_Ardashir_I_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian Empire was founded in 224 CE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a>, a local ruler from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pars">Pars</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fars">Fars</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a>) and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> of the Arsacid Parthian dynasty, defeated and killed the last Parthian king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#artabanus-iv">Artabanus IV</a>, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hormozdgan">Battle of Hormozdgan</a>, before taking the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shahanshah">Shahanshah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#negus-nagast">King of Kings</a>, and entering the former Parthian capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tigris">Tigris</a> around 226. Ardashir's family traced its descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sasan">Sasan</a>, traditionally a priest at the temple of Anahita at Istakhr, from whom the dynasty took its name, and this priestly lineage supplied both religious legitimacy and the ideological programme that distinguished the new empire from its predecessor. Where the Arsacids had ruled through a loose confederacy of semi-autonomous noble houses, Ardashir pursued centralisation, curbing the great clans, reorganising the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iranian-plateau">Iranian plateau</a> and elevating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> as the state-sponsored religion, supported by a network of royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fire-temple">fire temples</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a> (r. c. 240–270 CE) consolidated and greatly expanded the state, waging three major wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> that culminated in the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#valerian">Emperor Valerian</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-edessa">Battle of Edessa</a> in 260 CE, the only occasion on which a reigning Roman emperor was taken prisoner by a foreign power, an event commemorated in the rock relief at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, where the captive Valerian appears before the mounted king beside the kneeling Philip the Arab. The enabling conditions for the Sasanian revolution were the chronic dynastic instability of the late Parthian period, the ambitions of the Pars aristocracy under Arsacid overlordship, and Ardashir's ability to combine military capability with a coherent ideology rooted in Zoroastrian kingship and the claim to be restoring the imperial tradition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenids</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#bishapur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bishapur.jpg" alt="Bishapur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#bishapur">Bishapur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anahita_Temple_of_Bishapur_(Kazerun)_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Behnam9395, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sasanian society was organised according to a hierarchy of estates formalised in Zoroastrian tradition: priests (<em>asronan</em>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asravan">asravan</a>), warriors (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#arteshtaran">arteshtaran</a></em>), scribes and administrators (<em>dibiran</em>, also <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dabiran">dabiran</a>), and commoners, divided into peasant cultivators (<em>wastaryoshan</em>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vastryoshan">vastryoshan</a>) and artisans (<em>hutukhshan</em>). This framework was not merely theoretical: mobility between estates was restricted and occupational identity was largely hereditary. The agricultural economy of the Mesopotamian lowlands, the empire's wealthiest region, depended on an elaborate infrastructure of canals, dykes and water-management works, some of them built by Roman prisoners of war captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a>'s campaigns; Shapur's foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishapur">Bishapur</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#band-e-kaisar">Band-e Kaisar</a> dam across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karun-river">Karun River</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shushtar">Shushtar</a>, both associated with Roman captive labour and expertise, exemplify this deliberate exploitation of captured knowledge. Mesopotamian farms produced wheat, barley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rice">rice</a>, dates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sesame">sesame</a> in quantities sufficient to sustain dense urban populations and generate a substantial taxable surplus. The fiscal system rested on a land tax (<em>harag</em>) assessed on agricultural production and a poll tax (<em>gazidag</em>) levied on commoners, from which nobles, priests, soldiers and scribes were exempt, supplemented by customs duties and royal monopolies. The empire's position at the junction of the overland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk-road">Silk Road</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>, the maritime route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and the routes westward to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> generated commercial revenues that successive rulers sought to maximise through military control of chokepoints and diplomacy with trading partners from China to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shapur-i.jpg" alt="Shapur I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shapour_I_statue.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown after Rawlinson, George, 1812-1902, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian military evolved considerably over the empire's 427-year existence but relied throughout on the heavily armoured cavalry of the great noble houses as its premier strike force, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#war-elephant">war elephants</a>, infantry levies and, from the fifth century, increasingly professional units. The <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#savaran">savaran</a></em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> in which both horse and rider could be clad in mail and lamellar armour, were among the most formidable mounted forces of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eurasia">Eurasia</a> and strongly influenced the development of the Roman and Byzantine <em>clibanarii</em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kataphraktoi">kataphraktoi</a></em>. Sasanian siege craft, learned partly from captured Roman specialists and refined over centuries of war against fortified Roman frontier cities, reached a high level of sophistication, enabling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-i">Shapur I</a>'s capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antioch">Antioch</a> in the 250s, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shapur-ii">Shapur II</a>'s seizure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amida">Amida</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diyarbakir">Diyarbakir</a>) in 359 CE after a 73-day siege, and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>'s general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> in 614 CE. A signal achievement was Shapur II's reversal of the humiliating peace imposed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> in 299 CE: after decades of war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> and Julian, Shapur obtained, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peace-of-363">Peace of 363</a> concluded with Jovian after Julian's death on campaign, the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nisibis">Nisibis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#singara">Singara</a>, five provinces beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tigris">Tigris</a> and fifteen fortresses, the most favourable settlement <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a> ever won from Rome. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> Anushirvan reorganised the army on a more professional footing, dividing high command among four regional generals (<em>spahbed</em>) and paying and equipping cavalrymen from the reformed treasury, and in alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk Khaganate</a> destroyed the Hephthalite empire (c. 557–560 CE) that had long threatened the north-eastern frontier, carrying Sasanian influence towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oxus">Amu Darya</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khosrow-i.jpg" alt="Khosrow I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_of_the_Sasanian_king_Khosrow_I_Anushirvan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian administration combined the centralising ambitions of its founders with the practical need to accommodate the great noble houses (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wuzurgan">wuzurgan</a></em>), whose military power and regional authority could never be entirely suppressed. At the apex stood the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#shahanshah">Shahanshah</a>, whose authority was theoretically absolute and sanctioned through the Zoroastrian concept of <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khvarenah">xwarrah</a></em>, the divine royal glory that legitimised rule and was believed to abandon unworthy kings. Below him the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wuzurg-framadar">wuzurg framadar</a></em> (grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizier</a>) coordinated central administration, while the empire was divided into provinces (<em>shahr</em>), with frontier regions under <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#marzban">marzban</a></em> (margraves) responsible for both civil administration and defence. The Zoroastrian priestly hierarchy, headed by the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mobedan-mobed">mobedan mobed</a></em> (chief priest), exercised authority over religious law, education and the ideological apparatus of the state, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fire-temple">fire temple</a> network functioned at once as a religious institution, an administrative centre and the keeper of the sacred fires whose maintenance symbolised dynastic legitimacy. Sasanian law is best known from the <em>Madayan i Hazar Dadestan</em> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#matigan-i-hazar-datistan">Book of a Thousand Judgements</a>), a compilation of the early seventh century that reveals a sophisticated jurisprudential tradition addressing contracts, property, inheritance and family relations with considerable technical precision. The most significant administrative reform of the period was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>'s replacement, building on initiatives of his father Kavad I, of the traditional harvest-share tax, which required annual assessment and invited official corruption, with a fixed land tax based on surveys of cultivated area, crop type and irrigation, a reform that produced more stable revenues and survived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a> into the fiscal systems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#athens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Parlette, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian Empire's influence on later Eurasian history operated through religious, cultural, administrative and military channels, and proved remarkably durable despite the empire's political extinction in 651 CE. Most consequential was the transmission of Persian administrative, artistic and intellectual traditions into the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphates</a>, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>: Persian secretaries and scholars staffed the Abbasid government from its foundation, New Persian eventually rivalled Arabic as a language of administration and high culture across the eastern Islamic world, and Sasanian motifs such as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simurgh">simurgh</a></em>, the royal hunt and the enthroned audience scene permeated Islamic decorative arts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#andalusia">Andalusia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-asia">Central Asia</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a> the court became a centre of cross-cultural scholarship: the king welcomed Neoplatonist philosophers who left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a> after Justinian's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#closure-of-the-neoplatonic-academy">closure of the Neoplatonic Academy</a> in 529 CE, sponsored translations from Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, and fostered medical learning at Gundishapur, whose later reputation as the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#academy-of-gundishapur">Academy of Gundishapur</a> is probably exaggerated but whose physicians did serve the early Abbasid court, helping to transmit a synthesis of Greek, Indian and Iranian learning into the Islamic scientific tradition of the eighth to tenth centuries. Sasanian military practice, particularly heavily armoured cavalry and mounted archery, influenced both the Byzantine army and the warrior traditions of Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a>, though reduced to a minority faith in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iran">Iran</a> after the conquest, survived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parsis">Parsi</a> communities of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a>, descendants of Zoroastrian migrants from Iran, and continues as a living tradition whose cultural influence far exceeds its small numbers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-al-qadisiyyah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-al-qadisiyyah.jpg" alt="Battle of al-Qadisiyyah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Depiction_of_the_Battle_of_al-Q%C4%81disiyyah_from_a_manuscript_of_the_Persian_epic_Sh%C4%81h-n%C4%81meh._Source-_British_Library_(MS._I.O.Islamic_3265_(1614)_f._602r).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artistUnknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Sasanian collapse between 628 and 651 CE was among the most rapid and complete in ancient history, transforming the dominant power of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> into conquered territory within little more than two decades, yet its causes had been accumulating across a century of intermittent war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>. The proximate trigger was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-sasanian-war-of-602-628">Byzantine-Sasanian War</a> of 602–628 CE, launched after the emperor Maurice, who had helped restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> to power against the usurper Bahram Chobin in 591 CE, was overthrown and murdered by the junior officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#phocas">Phocas</a>. Khosrow II seized upon Maurice's murder as a pretext for the most ambitious Sasanian offensive in centuries: from 603 his armies overran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenia">Armenia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#palestine">Palestine</a>, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> by around 621, and twice reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bosphorus">Bosphorus</a> opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>, culminating in the siege of 626, achieving an extent that rivalled the Achaemenid empire. This overextension proved fatal: the treasury was drained by continuous campaigning, the peasantry was heavily taxed to sustain armies across enormous distances, and the administration was strained beyond its capacity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#heraclius">Heraclius</a>'s counter-offensive from 622 CE reversed these gains within six years, culminating in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nineveh">Nineveh</a> on 12 December 627 CE; Khosrow II was then deposed and killed in a coup led by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kavad-ii">Kavad II</a> in 628, opening a succession crisis in which perhaps a dozen rulers claimed the throne within four years, among them the queens <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#boran">Boran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#azarmidokht">Azarmidokht</a>, none of whom could command the loyalty of the great noble houses or the army. This disintegration left the empire without effective central authority just as the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> invaded in 633 CE; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-al-qadisiyyah">Battle of al-Qadisiyyah</a> (c. 636 CE) destroyed the main Sasanian field army, and the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a>, fleeing eastward as province after province submitted, was murdered near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#merv">Merv</a> in 651 CE, traditionally by a local miller, while his son Peroz sought refuge at the Tang court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Sasanian Empire represents the culmination of pre-Islamic Iranian civilisation, and its administrative traditions, artistic achievements and intellectual life shaped the culture of the Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphates</a> that succeeded it so thoroughly that Islamic civilisation cannot be understood without recognising the Sasanian foundations on which it was built.</em></p>

